57
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:26,420
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

58
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:40,459
What's the role of a troop committee, and what is the proper relationship in the leadership

59
00:03:40,459 --> 00:03:46,079
structure of a scout troop for the troop committee and the other two leadership groups?

60
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:53,679
That would be the Scoutmaster and his assistants, and then the Patrol Leaders Council, or all

61
00:03:53,679 --> 00:03:54,700
the youth leaders in the troop.

62
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:57,420
There are three kind of distinct groups within a scout troop.

63
00:03:58,060 --> 00:04:02,079
What are the proper relationships between these three groups?

64
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:07,239
Concentrating specifically on the committee role for a moment.

65
00:04:07,799 --> 00:04:12,519
Well, a good place to start to answer that question is to really understand what happens

66
00:04:12,519 --> 00:04:14,500
at the level of a troop committee.

67
00:04:14,739 --> 00:04:20,140
I recommend to you the online training that the BSA offers on the subject.

68
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:25,320
That'll give you a really good idea of exactly what the functions of a troop committee are

69
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:27,940
and how it's supposed to function.

70
00:04:28,719 --> 00:04:30,180
It's a little thin.

71
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:36,479
That particular training is a little thin on the relationship of the troop committee to the

72
00:04:36,479 --> 00:04:40,659
Scoutmaster and his assistants and also to the youth leadership.

73
00:04:41,080 --> 00:04:43,340
That's what I want to kind of expand on here.

74
00:04:43,340 --> 00:04:46,500
The broad concepts are what I think is very important.

75
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:54,640
One missing emphasis in the training is that the central decision-making group, as far as

76
00:04:54,640 --> 00:05:00,000
the program and activities that Scouts are going to do, is the Patrol Leaders Council.

77
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,520
The Patrol Leaders Council sets their calendar.

78
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:07,020
They decide what they want to do.

79
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:16,940
And then that, through the Scoutmaster, goes to the troop committee for its support and approval.

80
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:19,940
That is basically the structure.

81
00:05:21,060 --> 00:05:24,880
And that's the way the three relationships are outlined.

82
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:32,600
The role of the Scoutmaster and his assistants is to coach and to train and to work with the

83
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:38,800
youth leadership and then to represent them to the troop committee who lends their plans.

84
00:05:39,020 --> 00:05:43,600
I'm talking about the Patrol Leaders Council's plans, administrative support.

85
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:47,340
It's an important relationship to get right.

86
00:05:47,340 --> 00:05:55,760
Now, what usually happens is that a troop is somewhere on the continuum from being totally

87
00:05:55,760 --> 00:06:02,039
adult-led, where the boys are merely participants in a program being presented by the adults, to

88
00:06:02,039 --> 00:06:09,320
being totally youth-led, where the youth are planning and presenting their own program and activities.

89
00:06:09,979 --> 00:06:13,620
So our goal is to be on the youth-led side of that continuum.

90
00:06:14,120 --> 00:06:15,780
And we're somewhere in the middle.

91
00:06:15,780 --> 00:06:20,919
Now, why isn't every troop just naturally youth-led?

92
00:06:21,580 --> 00:06:28,820
And how does it come to be an adult-led kind of idea?

93
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:32,020
One answer is that adults don't get trained.

94
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:34,559
And they don't understand the program.

95
00:06:35,099 --> 00:06:39,619
So they don't understand the relationship between these three leadership structures within a Scout troop.

96
00:06:39,679 --> 00:06:43,140
And they don't understand exactly what Scouts are supposed to be doing.

97
00:06:43,140 --> 00:06:50,360
And so they kind of default to this thing where adults plan and present and boys participate,

98
00:06:50,740 --> 00:06:54,240
kind of as guests at something that the adults are putting on.

99
00:06:55,060 --> 00:07:00,940
Or somebody gets trained and gets the idea, oh, well, we're supposed to be youth-led,

100
00:07:01,039 --> 00:07:02,640
so let's work on that.

101
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,800
And the first couple of times out of the box, it's a little shaky, and the youth don't make

102
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:07,560
very good plans.

103
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:09,800
I mean, their plans are more like a puddle than a lake.

104
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:12,620
And it's deeply frustrating to adults.

105
00:07:13,540 --> 00:07:14,820
Very deeply frustrating.

106
00:07:14,940 --> 00:07:16,720
Well, they didn't plan this, and they didn't plan that.

107
00:07:16,780 --> 00:07:18,620
They didn't think of this, and they didn't think of that.

108
00:07:18,660 --> 00:07:20,580
And this patrol didn't have any food.

109
00:07:20,580 --> 00:07:28,219
And so the adults get very frustrated, and they slowly but surely begin to take a lot of the

110
00:07:28,219 --> 00:07:33,120
responsibility away from the Scouts, take it on as their own, and they get a little meddlesome

111
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:34,020
and coercive.

112
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:40,940
And finally, the Scouts give up because Scout-age boys are going to follow the path of least resistance

113
00:07:40,940 --> 00:07:48,120
out of a sense of respect for their elders in one way and a sense of unfamiliarity with

114
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:49,820
the process in another.

115
00:07:51,060 --> 00:07:59,980
So a troop can quickly become something that is on the other end of the continuum where

116
00:07:59,980 --> 00:08:06,940
the adults are taking over and kind of meddling, and they are presenting and administering a

117
00:08:06,940 --> 00:08:13,300
program for boys that boys should really be creating and presenting and administering for

118
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:13,800
themselves.

119
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:23,520
So the first thing to get right, in my mind, is where is the principal decision-making done

120
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:25,400
as to what the troop will do?

121
00:08:25,520 --> 00:08:27,360
That is at the Patrol Leaders' Council.

122
00:08:28,220 --> 00:08:34,860
Now, this is not a set of responsibilities that a bunch of benevolent adults decide they

123
00:08:34,860 --> 00:08:36,740
will give to Scouts.

124
00:08:36,740 --> 00:08:43,320
This is one that the Scout, this is a set of responsibilities that the Scouts actually

125
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:43,760
own.

126
00:08:45,180 --> 00:08:49,660
They own, as the program is written, these are things that the Scouts own.

127
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:59,240
And it's an imbalance and it's a disservice to Scouting as a program generally and to a Scout

128
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:07,180
troop specifically for adults to usurp those responsibilities that are properly in the hands of Scouts.

129
00:09:07,560 --> 00:09:15,060
Even though it's, as I said, really, really frustrating to adults when they are basically

130
00:09:15,060 --> 00:09:17,620
working with boys who don't know how to plan things.

131
00:09:18,340 --> 00:09:20,640
So what's the answer?

132
00:09:20,640 --> 00:09:28,460
Well, the Scoutmaster and their assistants need to coach and to plan without a great deal of

133
00:09:28,460 --> 00:09:32,140
coercion, without taking things over.

134
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:36,180
They need to coach and plan along with the Patrol Leaders' Council.

135
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:44,900
They need to make sure that those leaders are being trained in the idea of leading themselves.

136
00:09:44,900 --> 00:09:55,100
And that helps to create a balance between the three groups of leadership within a Scout troop.

137
00:09:55,280 --> 00:09:59,760
People often write to me and they say, well, we're working really hard on trying to make

138
00:09:59,760 --> 00:10:01,240
this a boy-run troop.

139
00:10:01,740 --> 00:10:09,120
Well, there's one thing and one thing alone that stands in the way of having the youth leaders

140
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:16,360
actually lead the troop, and that is one or more adults who simply won't let them do it

141
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:17,880
or just don't get it.

142
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:21,620
That's the one thing that stands in the way.

143
00:10:22,200 --> 00:10:24,920
It's not laziness on the part of the boys.

144
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:26,800
They're not being lazy.

145
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:27,460
They just don't.

146
00:10:27,700 --> 00:10:30,980
You can't be lazy about something that you have no idea about.

147
00:10:31,420 --> 00:10:33,960
They've never seen a model and they don't know how to do it.

148
00:10:33,960 --> 00:10:44,520
So, our first job, really, is adult leader training that stresses the autonomy of the

149
00:10:44,520 --> 00:10:48,740
Patrol Leaders' Council to make these decisions and the role of adults in supporting them.

150
00:10:49,220 --> 00:10:53,500
If the adults are leading and presenting everything, well, that will seem quite natural.

151
00:10:53,900 --> 00:10:55,280
Youth members are leading everything.

152
00:10:55,620 --> 00:10:57,240
That will seem quite natural.

153
00:10:57,240 --> 00:11:01,980
So, both of those ways of doing it, either end of the continuum and all the points in

154
00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:06,940
between, have a way of perpetuating themselves over time because people look at it and they

155
00:11:06,940 --> 00:11:07,960
say, well, this is the way it goes.

156
00:11:08,040 --> 00:11:13,940
And even if they go to training, somebody will say something like, well, you go to training

157
00:11:13,940 --> 00:11:17,960
and you get trained, but you have to do things that work and this is the way it works for

158
00:11:17,960 --> 00:11:18,300
us.

159
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:26,280
And, you know, all those things, to me, are just any number of excuses and equivocations.

160
00:11:27,060 --> 00:11:32,219
We understand the scout program, but we don't actually apply it because it would be inconvenient

161
00:11:32,219 --> 00:11:33,439
and difficult for us.

162
00:11:33,599 --> 00:11:39,500
My encouragement to you is to really think about the broad concept of the way the three

163
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:44,920
bodies of leadership in any scout troop, that's the scoutmaster and his assistants, who are the

164
00:11:44,920 --> 00:11:54,060
coaches and mentors of the Patrol Leaders Council, who is the central decision-making body of

165
00:11:54,060 --> 00:11:57,979
the troop as far as program and activities are involved.

166
00:11:58,240 --> 00:12:03,079
And then the troop committee, which is a source of administrative support, of vetting and appointment

167
00:12:03,079 --> 00:12:10,040
of scout leaders, doing things that are beyond, far beyond the scope of a Patrol Leaders Council.

168
00:12:10,040 --> 00:12:18,240
So hopefully that helps kind of set you up with some broad concepts in the way that a troop

169
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:22,980
committee relates to the other two leadership organizations within a troop.

170
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:27,900
I'm a little deep, I'm short and stout.

171
00:12:28,319 --> 00:12:31,620
Here is my handle, here is my spout.

172
00:12:32,099 --> 00:12:35,360
When I get all steamed up, then I shout.

173
