Tip me over and pull me out.

183
00:13:04,640 --> 00:13:06,440
Our patrols keep a patrol log.

184
00:13:06,540 --> 00:13:10,300
And at summer camp, it's especially useful because it kind of gives them a report on the

185
00:13:10,300 --> 00:13:14,480
day that they can bring to the senior patrol leader at our evening Patrol Leaders Council

186
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:14,900
meeting.

187
00:13:15,620 --> 00:13:16,600
So here we are.

188
00:13:16,840 --> 00:13:22,560
We're sitting around at night of a, you know, a sultry summer camp day and the bugs are chirping

189
00:13:22,560 --> 00:13:24,020
and carrying on.

190
00:13:24,020 --> 00:13:28,180
And, you know, I've got this one very young new patrol leader.

191
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:37,319
He's kind of acting like, you know, a new employee would act in a board meeting or something

192
00:13:37,319 --> 00:13:39,780
like that, being very deferential and kind of quiet.

193
00:13:39,780 --> 00:13:45,500
You know, when it came his turn to talk about what was in his patrol log for the day, he

194
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:53,699
mentioned that one of the boys in the patrol was somewhat concerned about the snake that

195
00:13:53,699 --> 00:13:56,220
was living under his tent platform.

196
00:14:09,780 --> 00:14:16,640
Now, you've got to understand, where we go camping, there's, you know, snakes.

197
00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:17,959
There's garter snakes.

198
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:21,140
There's a very rare roughneck green snake.

199
00:14:21,780 --> 00:14:22,939
There's rat snakes.

200
00:14:24,260 --> 00:14:25,860
There's several different kinds of snake.

201
00:14:25,959 --> 00:14:30,040
But most importantly, there's one that we have to pay particular attention to, and that's

202
00:14:30,040 --> 00:14:30,640
a copperhead.

203
00:14:30,939 --> 00:14:34,000
If you're not familiar with copperheads, copperheads are poisonous.

204
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:39,840
They're not going to out now kill you, but it's certainly unpleasant to be bitten by one.

205
00:14:40,340 --> 00:14:48,100
So as the patrol leader sat there and he said rather quietly, yeah, you know, Alex is a little

206
00:14:48,100 --> 00:14:50,980
concerned about the snake that lives under his tent.

207
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:58,000
It kind of caught my attention.

208
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:10,980
After the patrol leader's council meeting, I grabbed one of my assistant scoutmasters,

209
00:15:11,420 --> 00:15:16,960
and I said, let's go look at the snake that's living under Alex's tent.

210
00:15:17,380 --> 00:15:18,700
It might be a good idea.

211
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:23,980
And so anyway, is, you know, we're sleeping on tent platforms.

212
00:15:24,120 --> 00:15:25,000
They're wooden platforms.

213
00:15:25,140 --> 00:15:26,420
The tents are set on the top.

214
00:15:26,820 --> 00:15:31,840
And the scouts are headed off to the shower house before they hit the hay for the evening.

215
00:15:31,840 --> 00:15:34,680
And I thought, you know, we'll just go down very quietly.

216
00:15:34,800 --> 00:15:37,620
We'll take a look at this and see exactly what's going on.

217
00:15:37,740 --> 00:15:38,640
So here we are.

218
00:15:38,760 --> 00:15:42,100
We're down on our hands and knees kind of looking around underneath the tent platform.

219
00:15:42,300 --> 00:15:43,080
And there he is.

220
00:15:43,120 --> 00:15:43,700
It's a snake.

221
00:15:44,020 --> 00:15:45,800
And guess what kind of snake it is?

222
00:15:46,040 --> 00:15:46,760
Can you guess?

223
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:47,540
Huh?

224
00:15:52,780 --> 00:15:53,360
Oh, yeah.

225
00:15:53,700 --> 00:15:56,020
Yeah, it's a little, it's a little copperhead.

226
00:15:56,020 --> 00:16:02,939
You know, these, I've dealt with these guys before and they're, you want to treat them with respect and things.

227
00:16:03,280 --> 00:16:09,000
So I asked one of the fellows who is on the camp staff who was down in the site with us, he's one of our scouts.

228
00:16:09,160 --> 00:16:12,640
I say, you know, go on up, find the nature director.

229
00:16:13,160 --> 00:16:17,660
Tell him we got a snake and tell him to bring the snake stick and stuff down there.

230
00:16:17,660 --> 00:16:26,620
You know, they've got to, you know, we wait about 10 or 15 minutes and, and the nature director comes running breathlessly into the, into the campsite.

231
00:16:26,660 --> 00:16:36,520
He said, and in, in rather a louder voice than I would have hoped, he talks about the copperhead and we've got to capture this copperhead.

232
00:16:36,560 --> 00:16:42,140
And I'm like, yeah, well, let's, we might be able to do this without causing a whole lot of craziness.

233
00:16:42,140 --> 00:16:46,000
So, um, let's just keep it quiet. Oh, yeah. Okay.

234
00:16:51,100 --> 00:16:56,560
So we begin trying to kind of, you know, reach under the platform and get the snake.

235
00:16:56,640 --> 00:16:59,040
And of course the snake is not very interested in this.

236
00:16:59,760 --> 00:17:01,580
Um, the snake would rather stay where he's at.

237
00:17:01,640 --> 00:17:03,819
He's comfortable. He's got a couple of scouts right there.

238
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:06,780
Should he need to bite them, poison them, um, for any reason.

239
00:17:06,780 --> 00:17:12,960
So, uh, he's really got a pretty good crib right there and, uh, he's not very interested in moving.

240
00:17:13,300 --> 00:17:15,000
One thing leads to another, right?

241
00:17:15,260 --> 00:17:21,579
And finally there's, you know, uh, five, uh, adults and, and, and scouts and 10 flashlights.

242
00:17:21,819 --> 00:17:23,700
And there's a lot of hollering.

243
00:17:25,119 --> 00:17:31,760
So our quiet little snake extraction turns into, you know, a major, um, a major operation.

244
00:17:31,760 --> 00:17:40,820
And we, we've completely disassembled the tent platform to get at this snake and, uh, finally get it in the snake stick after a whole lot of excitement.

245
00:17:41,600 --> 00:17:51,700
And so, uh, me and the nature director walk on down the trail to a remote part of camp to, uh, release the snake and, uh, just, you know, let him be.

246
00:17:51,800 --> 00:17:53,920
He'll be out there where he won't bother anybody.

247
00:17:53,920 --> 00:17:57,660
And, uh, so I thought, you know, great end of story.

248
00:17:58,080 --> 00:17:59,920
The boys won't be concerned about this copperhead.

249
00:18:00,560 --> 00:18:07,640
Of course, they'll be concerned that there is a copperhead now under every tent platform because we didn't manage to do it very quietly.

250
00:18:07,640 --> 00:18:12,800
But we all headed off to bed and, uh, I had kind of figured that we were done.

251
00:18:18,880 --> 00:18:20,180
But we weren't.

252
00:18:20,200 --> 00:18:21,460
No, of course we weren't.

253
00:18:21,460 --> 00:18:26,880
You know, one of the great things about being in camp with the scouts is that any little piece of news travels very quickly.

254
00:18:27,120 --> 00:18:29,480
Uh, in camp we call it the moccasin telegraph.

255
00:18:29,900 --> 00:18:31,360
And pretty soon the whole camp knows.

256
00:18:31,840 --> 00:18:34,060
And then the story gets embroidered and embellished.

257
00:18:34,340 --> 00:18:35,160
So here we are.

258
00:18:35,240 --> 00:18:37,140
We're walking back from lunch the next day.

259
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:43,300
I get back to the campsite and one of the, my assistant leaders, uh, catches up with me.

260
00:18:43,700 --> 00:18:47,340
And he says, yeah, he says, uh, there's quite a story about the snake last night.

261
00:18:47,360 --> 00:18:47,880
And I say, really?

262
00:18:47,960 --> 00:18:50,060
He says, yeah, well, this is the story right now.

263
00:18:50,060 --> 00:18:54,540
Because as he was walking back from lunch, he heard scouts from another troop discussing this.

264
00:19:02,379 --> 00:19:10,700
So the story was that there was this huge copperhead coiled up on the bed, not under the platform,

265
00:19:10,700 --> 00:19:13,420
but on the bed of one of the young scouts in our troop.

266
00:19:13,520 --> 00:19:14,760
And it was attacking them.

267
00:19:15,440 --> 00:19:18,180
And the scoutmaster had to kill it with an axe.

268
00:19:19,900 --> 00:19:25,340
And then had apparently skinned it and made a hat band, which he would be sporting at that night's, uh, retreat ceremony.

269
00:19:25,880 --> 00:19:29,080
So, you know, that's the way the moccasin telegraph works, isn't it?

270
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:41,100
And, uh, it has tempted me before to create some stories, but, uh, I've managed to resist the temptation.

271
00:19:41,240 --> 00:19:42,240
I'll tell them to you here.

272
00:19:42,240 --> 00:20:12,220
I'll tell them to you here.

273
00:20:12,240 --> 00:20:28,320
So this is the second in our series of talking about a troop-based high adventure trip.

274
00:20:28,460 --> 00:20:36,220
Talk a little bit about, uh, what a high adventure trip is and what the rationale for building one is in our last podcast.

275
00:20:36,580 --> 00:20:42,120
And this time we're talking about, um, some more of the nuts and bolts and the details that get involved.

276
00:20:42,240 --> 00:20:44,340
I mean, where do you go and what do you do?

277
00:20:44,900 --> 00:20:48,280
Well, the simple answer to that question is just about anywhere and just about anything.

278
00:20:49,460 --> 00:20:51,240
What are your scouts interested in doing?

279
00:20:51,600 --> 00:20:53,320
Are they interested in a week of backpacking?

280
00:20:53,860 --> 00:20:55,180
Uh, maybe canoeing?

281
00:20:55,460 --> 00:20:56,840
Uh, maybe just touring.

282
00:20:56,840 --> 00:21:07,360
There's nothing to say that you don't rent a van and you go visit, uh, several historic sites or something like that, uh, during, during the time that you, uh, set aside for a high adventure trip.

283
00:21:07,459 --> 00:21:10,320
Maybe they're interested in cycling or maybe they're interested in boating.

284
00:21:10,659 --> 00:21:12,699
Or, you know, you choose.

285
00:21:13,199 --> 00:21:17,659
Making a trip happen requires four things.

286
00:21:17,660 --> 00:21:20,680
The first is vision and leadership.

287
00:21:21,580 --> 00:21:25,240
That's easy to say, but it's more important than you might think.

288
00:21:25,760 --> 00:21:28,020
Somebody has to say, let's go.

289
00:21:28,940 --> 00:21:33,960
And then they have to show the other folks in the troop that it's actually possible.

290
00:21:35,280 --> 00:21:40,120
This can be a little more challenging than you think, especially with your first troop-based trip.

291
00:21:40,620 --> 00:21:42,160
But I'll tell you something.

292
00:21:42,280 --> 00:21:44,200
From experience, it's possible.

293
00:21:44,200 --> 00:21:47,440
It's not only possible, it's not as hard as you might think.

294
00:21:47,880 --> 00:21:49,840
It's not only not as hard as you might think.

295
00:21:50,160 --> 00:21:53,280
It gets to be easy the more trips that you do.

296
00:21:54,800 --> 00:21:57,380
The second thing out of four that it takes is details.

297
00:21:59,220 --> 00:22:01,360
Troop-based trips are full of details.

298
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:08,180
And preparation time is going to take at least as many hours as the trip itself and probably a little bit more.

299
00:22:08,180 --> 00:22:17,020
But once you consign yourself to the fact that you're going to be doing a lot of detail work, then it's not quite so onerous.

300
00:22:18,320 --> 00:22:21,120
Third is a commitment to training and skill development.

301
00:22:21,600 --> 00:22:33,040
Now, whoever's going to lead or advise participants has to commit time to training and learning the applicable skills to safely carry out the plan.

302
00:22:33,040 --> 00:22:37,060
And fourth is going to be finances.

303
00:22:37,760 --> 00:22:45,220
Now, calculating the cost of the trip and collecting money and paying it out and accounting for all of that is an important aspect.

304
00:22:45,500 --> 00:22:46,899
There might be fundraising with it.

305
00:22:47,500 --> 00:22:49,540
It's all very important stuff.

306
00:22:50,040 --> 00:22:53,620
And those are the four things that we're going to talk about.

307
00:22:53,879 --> 00:22:56,180
The first, vision and leadership.

308
00:22:56,520 --> 00:22:59,940
It's the most important step because without any vision, you're not going anywhere.

309
00:22:59,940 --> 00:23:10,220
It's important to establish and protect the vision and to build anticipation and excitement with it because the details and the questions are going to try and kill the vision.

310
00:23:10,480 --> 00:23:15,140
If we want to motivate people, we need to get a picture of exactly what we're going to do.

311
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:23,000
And one of our first canoe trips to Canada, we sat around our campfire on the first night after getting to a very hard-won campsite.

312
00:23:23,100 --> 00:23:27,760
And it was dark and it was difficult to get ourselves there.

313
00:23:27,760 --> 00:23:35,940
A day of paddling and portaging and a frustrating search for the campsite and, you know, the crew got separated, had me pretty wrung out.

314
00:23:36,100 --> 00:23:44,140
And I was lamenting over a couple of mistakes that I made to one of my fellow adult leaders who was sitting there at the fire making our dinner.

315
00:23:44,720 --> 00:23:49,480
And he looked at me kind of incredulously and he said, hey, look, here we are.

316
00:23:49,620 --> 00:23:52,360
We're camped out on a lake in Canada and we're going to have a steak dinner.

317
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:54,040
I mean, what more do you want?

318
00:23:54,040 --> 00:23:59,560
So he had maintained the vision while I was bogged down in details.

319
00:24:00,159 --> 00:24:07,000
Getting that first troop high adventure trip off the ground required helping my scouts envision it as a real possibility.

320
00:24:07,159 --> 00:24:09,360
I was surprised at how skeptical they were.

321
00:24:10,260 --> 00:24:14,800
I guess it all seemed a little too good to be true or too logistically challenging to launch.

322
00:24:15,520 --> 00:24:18,800
The adults in the troop also required a bit of convincing.

323
00:24:19,800 --> 00:24:22,460
You know, some questioned if we were allowed to do such things.

324
00:24:22,460 --> 00:24:24,680
Some were concerned about the cost.

325
00:24:24,900 --> 00:24:27,060
Some were concerned about safety.

326
00:24:27,580 --> 00:24:30,340
And some were concerned about all three or four things like that.

327
00:24:30,820 --> 00:24:35,340
But they did finally get a hold of the idea and we were able to bring it into reality.

328
00:24:36,820 --> 00:24:47,340
So establishing the vision and then fanning the flames and motivating scouts to maintain their resolve through this forest of details is as important as the details themselves.

329
00:24:47,500 --> 00:24:51,580
Niels van der Rohe once said famously, God is in the details.

330
00:24:51,580 --> 00:24:53,540
So let's talk about the detail part of it.

331
00:24:53,919 --> 00:24:59,340
So the place that you're going to go, it might be a state or a provincial or a national park.

332
00:24:59,939 --> 00:25:01,580
And these are very likely destinations.

333
00:25:02,040 --> 00:25:04,659
And each is going to have its own particular rules and procedures.

334
00:25:05,639 --> 00:25:11,760
And, of course, getting online and getting an idea of exactly what you're in for is a good start.

335
00:25:11,760 --> 00:25:17,580
A lot of times, popular places will have a lot of online trip reports and reviews and those can be very informative.

336
00:25:18,400 --> 00:25:24,480
I'd suggest that once you think you have all the information that you can glean off the web, you pick up the phone and call someone at your destination.

337
00:25:24,840 --> 00:25:26,760
Find somebody who's interested in talking to you.

338
00:25:26,800 --> 00:25:31,180
Sometimes it'll be a park ranger or somebody who runs a concession in one of the parks.

339
00:25:31,560 --> 00:25:33,680
And confirm the information that you have is correct.

340
00:25:33,680 --> 00:25:41,720
Outfitters or other local businesses are usually more than happy to talk to you about putting a trip together.

341
00:25:42,520 --> 00:25:46,540
I would just get out and do and let the miles take care of themselves.

342
00:25:47,020 --> 00:25:52,400
But when you do start thinking about mileage, you need to, you know, have the context of terrain.

343
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:57,660
A mile that gains 2,000 feet in elevation is a lot different from a flat mile.

344
00:25:58,260 --> 00:26:04,340
The trail conditions, water, weather, and camping regulations, they'll affect the time and effort that are required to cover a distance.

345
00:26:04,500 --> 00:26:10,340
So if you're planning a hike as a part of your high adventure, you need to become very expert in these things.

346
00:26:10,520 --> 00:26:15,260
And you need to become expert in planning trail distances and route selection.

347
00:26:16,400 --> 00:26:20,820
And it's probably one of the more crucial choices in making or breaking your trip.

348
00:26:20,820 --> 00:26:26,800
And you need to gauge this route selection with the cruise ability and your familiarity with the terrain.

349
00:26:27,399 --> 00:26:28,860
The next detail is people.

350
00:26:29,659 --> 00:26:32,120
If you want, this isn't a rule.

351
00:26:32,419 --> 00:26:33,560
It's kind of a rule of thumb.

352
00:26:34,020 --> 00:26:36,860
Crews should probably be more than 5, less than 12.

353
00:26:37,840 --> 00:26:41,040
Now some destinations will regulate the maximum group size.

354
00:26:41,820 --> 00:26:44,020
Ratios of adults to youth are important.

355
00:26:44,020 --> 00:26:52,020
When we go canoeing, we usually have crews of 7 or 9 with the maximum and minimum of 3 adults over the age of 21.

356
00:26:53,100 --> 00:26:57,879
Because if we have an equal number of adults than scouts, or we have more adults than scouts,

357
00:26:58,060 --> 00:27:02,320
then the trip becomes diminished as far as the scout experience goes.

358
00:27:03,120 --> 00:27:05,800
The scouts should always outnumber the adults, in my opinion.

359
00:27:05,800 --> 00:27:11,720
I'm careful to explain that the adventure is for scouts, and adults are along for the ride.

360
00:27:12,940 --> 00:27:15,659
We make sure that our adults understand that.

361
00:27:15,740 --> 00:27:21,159
And sometimes we have to turn them away, because we already have the maximum number that we want to have.

362
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:26,100
So deciding who goes and how many, that's another important detail.

363
00:27:26,659 --> 00:27:30,139
Getting there and back, the transportation part of the trip, is another set of details.

364
00:27:30,140 --> 00:27:37,980
Rentals, and fares, and timetables, and drivers, and group rates, and insurance, and accommodations are all going to demand your attention.

365
00:27:38,360 --> 00:27:41,100
The gear that's going to be required, can it be rented?

366
00:27:41,740 --> 00:27:43,920
Or does it have to be purchased by your troop?

367
00:27:44,260 --> 00:27:46,140
Or does it get purchased by individuals?

368
00:27:46,280 --> 00:27:47,880
There's probably going to be a mix of all three.

369
00:27:48,480 --> 00:27:53,580
The quality and utility of your gear is important, and it will require a little bit of research.

370
00:27:53,580 --> 00:28:03,100
What looks inexpensive and indulgent at home may prove to be cheap and indispensable when you're, you know, eight hours away from the nearest store.

371
00:28:03,720 --> 00:28:11,320
So learning about the gear that's required and making sure that you get the best stuff that you can afford is very important.

372
00:28:11,960 --> 00:28:14,379
Food is another one of the details you're going to come up with.

373
00:28:14,939 --> 00:28:20,639
The appropriate weight, quantity, and nutritional value of a backcountry diet is a pretty big task,

374
00:28:20,939 --> 00:28:23,439
especially when you expand it into five or ten people.

375
00:28:23,580 --> 00:28:29,639
And then buying it and packing it after it's planned is also a pretty big task.

376
00:28:30,139 --> 00:28:37,699
In my experience, it's best to have at least two or three crew members assigned to doing different parts of that task and making sure that it happens.

377
00:28:38,340 --> 00:28:48,960
Training and skill development is important, and one of the skills that you're going to have is planning a menu and purchasing it for this kind of a trip.

378
00:28:48,960 --> 00:28:55,980
So those are the big detail areas that you're going to need to consider for a high adventure trip.

379
00:28:56,100 --> 00:29:03,620
And next time around, we're going to talk about making sure that everybody is trained and what the appropriate type of training is for a high adventure trip.

380
00:29:03,620 --> 00:29:04,620
Thank you.

381
00:29:04,620 --> 00:29:05,620
Thank you.

382
00:29:05,620 --> 00:29:06,620
Thank you.

383
00:29:06,620 --> 00:29:10,620
Thank you.

